# plain-language explanation for every figure: (how to read it, what it shows)
EXPL = {
1: ("Each dot is one lake, coloured by its average July surface temperature: yellow is warm, dark blue is cold. The dashed orange and dotted blue lines mark the two latitude bands compared later (the Alps band and southern Canada).",
    "Lakes are warmest across the southern United States, India, Southeast Asia and the Middle East, and coldest in the Arctic. The dark patch in Central Asia, at about 35°N, is the Tibetan Plateau: those lakes are as cold as Arctic lakes, even though they are as far south as the Mediterranean. That contrast is the question of this report in one picture. Most of the million lakes are in Canada, Scandinavia and Siberia."),
2: ("Each point is one zone of latitude. Panel a gives the altitude effect in that zone (°C per km of height) and panel b the latitude effect (°C per degree north). The three lines repeat the same calculation with zones 2.5°, 5° and 10° wide; shading is the 95% confidence range for the 5° zones.",
    "All three widths tell the same story: height cools lakes by about 3–4.5 °C per km at nearly every latitude, while latitude does almost nothing until about 35°N and then cools lakes by about 0.5–0.7 °C per degree. 2.5° zones are noisy and 10° zones hide detail, so 5° zones are used throughout."),
3: ("The horizontal axis is latitude, the vertical axis is elevation, and each square is the median July temperature of the lakes in that 1° × 100 m box. The black lines are isotherms, lines of equal temperature. A flat isotherm means only height changes the temperature; a vertical one means only latitude does; a slanted one means both do.",
    "South of about 33°N the isotherms run roughly flat: going north at the same height barely changes the temperature, but climbing does. North of 33°N they slope down to the right: to stay at the same temperature while moving north, a lake must be lower. The slope is about 130 m per degree of latitude. The empty space in the upper right exists because there are almost no high lakes in the far north."),
4: ("Each bar is the share of lake-to-lake differences in July temperature (R²) explained by latitude or altitude, either on its own or after the other has been taken into account.",
    "Latitude alone explains 38%; altitude alone explains almost nothing (0%). That is not because height doesn't matter: the high lakes are mostly at warm low latitudes (Tibet, Mexico, Ethiopia), so on average they are not colder than the huge number of low, cold lakes in Canada. Once latitude is accounted for, height explains a further 3.4%. Latitude dominates simply because lakes are spread across 70° of latitude but mostly sit low."),
5: ("One row per 5° zone. Each circle is the estimate in that zone and the bar its 95% confidence range: if a bar crosses the dashed zero line in panel b, the latitude effect there cannot be told apart from zero. Panel c is the predicted July temperature at sea level, in the middle of the zone, for a typical-depth lake.",
    "(a) Height cools lakes by about 3–4.5 °C per km in almost every zone; the dashed line is the value for the whole Alps band (−4.6). (b) Latitude has no effect, or even a slightly positive one, up to 35–40°N, then cools lakes by 0.5–0.7 °C per degree, and by more than 1 °C per degree at 65–70°N. (c) Sea-level lakes stay near 29–31 °C up to 40°N and then fall to about 16 °C at 65–70°N."),
6: ("A 2°-wide band was moved north one degree at a time. In panel a, each circle is the predicted July temperature at sea level in that band, and the two straight lines are the best two-line fit; the dashed line marks where they meet, with its 95% range shaded. Panel b shows the altitude effect in the same bands.",
    "Sea-level July temperature is flat, at about 29–31 °C, from the equator to 33.0°N (95% range 31.5–34.5°N), and then falls steadily, about 0.45 °C per degree, to 15 °C at 70°N. The altitude effect (b) has no such break: it stays near −3 to −4.5 °C per km and only becomes noisy north of 55°N, where few lakes are high."),
7: ("Two versions of the altitude effect in each 5° zone: one using July temperature, the other using each lake's average over all its ice-free days.",
    "The two agree up to about 25°N, where lakes are open all year. Further north they split: high lakes there are only open in summer, so their 'yearly' average is really a summer average and looks too warm, which makes height seem to matter less. This is why the report compares lakes in the same month (July) rather than using yearly averages. The agreement in the tropics also shows that the calculation itself is sound: the split further north comes from which months the lakes are open, not from the method."),
8: ("Four regions at the same latitude as the Alps (45.8–47.8°N). Each grey dot is one lake, plotted by height against July temperature; the orange line is the fitted cooling with height for a typical-depth lake.",
    "At the same latitude, lakes cool with height at very different rates: 7.2 °C per km in Europe, 5.3 in Central Asia, 4.0 in western North America and only 2.1 in East Asia. Latitude alone therefore cannot explain everything: the regional setting matters too. Europe has the tightest cloud of dots, because its lakes climb steadily from the lowlands to over 2,500 m in the Alps. East Asia has few high lakes and most of its lakes sit near sea level, so its line is the least certain (see the wide range in Figure 9)."),
9: ("Each circle is one region's cooling rate with height at the Alps latitude, with its 95% confidence range. The dashed line is the standard rate for air (−6.5 °C per km).",
    "Europe (−7.2) and Switzerland on its own (−6.8) cool at about the air's rate. Central Asia cools somewhat less, and western North America and East Asia much less, though their ranges are wide because they have fewer high lakes. The whole band together gives −4.6 °C per km. The regions differ more than chance would allow."),
10: ("Every lake in the Alps band, coloured by how far its real July temperature is from what the zone equation predicts: red lakes are warmer than predicted, blue lakes colder. Grey lines are country borders.",
    "The pattern is not random. Lakes near cold ocean (Newfoundland and the Gulf of St Lawrence, top right) and the glacier-fed lakes of the Alps are colder than predicted. Lakes deep inland, on the Ukrainian and Russian steppe and in the US interior west, are warmer. This is what led to distance from the ocean being added as a variable."),
11: ("Each point is the median July temperature of a group of lakes: low lakes (below 500 m) and high lakes (above 1,500 m) in the Alps band and in southern Canada. The number of lakes in each group is printed next to it.",
    "Moving 4.7° north, from the Alps band to southern Canada, costs about 2 °C at both heights. Climbing about 1.8 km costs about 7.7 °C in both bands. The two lines are nearly parallel, so the cost of height is the same at both latitudes. So one kilometre of height (about 4.3 °C) costs about twice as much as the whole move from the Alps to southern Canada."),
12: ("(a) How far lakes are from the predicted temperature (observed minus predicted) for lakes at different distances from the ocean, north and south of 33°N. (b) The Alps-band cooling rates from Figure 9 before (open circles) and after (filled) distance to the ocean is added to the equation. (c) Cooling rate with height for mountain lakes at different distances from the ocean.",
    "(a) North of 33°N, lakes within 10 km of the ocean are about 2.6 °C colder than predicted, and lakes 1,000 km inland about 2.5 °C warmer, a 5 °C swing from distance to the sea alone. South of 33°N the effect is small. (b) Once distance to the ocean is included, the two odd regions (East Asia and western North America) move toward the others. (c) Mountain lakes near the coast cool faster with height (about 6 °C per km) than inland mountain lakes (about 4.5–5 °C per km)."),
13: ("One panel per model. The horizontal axis is the real July temperature, the vertical axis the prediction for a lake the model had not seen. Colour shows how many lakes fall in each small square (log scale). A perfect model would put every lake on the dashed diagonal.",
    "Model 1 already follows the diagonal but with a wide cloud. Each step tightens it, and Model 4's cloud is narrow along almost the whole range. The remaining spread is largest for cold lakes (below about 10 °C), which are mostly Arctic and high-mountain lakes."),
14: ("For each model, the curve shows what share of lakes (vertical axis) is predicted within a given error (horizontal axis). A curve that rises faster and further to the left is a better model.",
    "Model 4 predicts 60% of lakes within ±1 °C and 87% within ±2 °C. The two models with the nearby-lakes correction (2 and 4) are clearly above the two without it, so the correction matters more than the extra variables in Model 3."),
15: ("Typical error (the median size of the miss) in each 5° band of latitude, for each model, on lakes not used to fit it.",
    "Errors grow toward the Arctic and jump just north of the 33°N split. The nearby-lakes correction (Models 2 and 4) removes most of this, flattening the curve to below 1 °C at almost every latitude."),
17: ("Black lines and circles are northern lakes in July; orange lines and squares are southern lakes in January (their summer). Panels a and b repeat Figure 5 for both hemispheres, panel c repeats Figure 6a, and panel d compares real southern temperatures (horizontal) with what the northern equation predicts (vertical).",
    "(a) The cooling with height is about the same in both hemispheres. (b) Near the equator, latitude does nothing in either hemisphere. (c) The south's break comes earlier, at 27.5° rather than 32.5° in this simple model, and the fall after it is steeper. (d) The northern equation, applied unchanged, still lands most southern lakes near the diagonal (R² 0.76) but runs about 1.5 °C too warm."),
16: ("(a) Each circle is one of the 158 lakes of 500 km² or more: real July temperature against the Model 1 prediction (open grey) and the prediction after the big-lake adjustment (filled orange). (b) Model 1's miss for each lake against its area, shaded by depth; the orange line is the adjustment.",
    "Without the adjustment almost every big lake sits above the diagonal: Model 1 makes them too warm, by 5.1 °C on average. The adjustment brings most of them onto the diagonal (2.6 °C). Panel b shows why: the miss grows with area, and deep lakes (black) miss the most, because a large volume of water warms slowly through the summer."),
18: ("(a) The median temperature of low lakes in each month, one line per 10° band of distance from the equator (southern lakes shifted by six months). (b) Yearly average and (c) summer–winter swing against distance from the equator; shading covers the middle half of lakes. (d) Swing against distance to the sea, for lakes 20–30° and 30–40° from the equator.",
    "Near the equator the line in (a) is almost flat: lakes stay near 28 °C all year. Further from the equator the curve becomes a tall hump. The yearly average (b) starts falling at 17.5°, well before July does (33°), and the swing (c) rises from almost nothing to about 25 °C at 35–40°N. At the same latitude, lakes far inland usually swing more (d), especially at 30–40°."),
19: ("(a) Days between mid-July and each lake's warmest point, by depth (line: median; shading: middle half of lakes). (b) Ice-free days per year for northern lakes that freeze, one line per height band. (c) Four lakes not used in fitting: dots are measured monthly temperatures, lines the year predicted from geography alone. (d) The average miss of the whole-year prediction in each month.",
    "(a) Lakes up to about 4 m deep peak in mid-July; a 50 m lake peaks about two weeks later. (b) The ice-free season shortens by about 9 days per degree north until 49.5°N, then more slowly, and every kilometre of height costs about a month. (c) Tonle Sap and Titicaca are well predicted, Geneva and Taupo less so: the model makes their winters too cold. (d) Errors are slightly larger in winter and spring (about 1.9 °C) than in late summer and autumn (about 1.4 °C)."),
}

